{"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Viewport"]]},"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Viewport\/anisotropicFilteringLevel"},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"SwiftGodot"}],"symbolKind":"property","title":"anisotropicFilteringLevel","fragments":[{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"anisotropicFilteringLevel","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Viewport","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:10SwiftGodot8ViewportC"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"AnisotropicFiltering","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:10SwiftGodot8ViewportC20AnisotropicFilteringO"}],"externalID":"s:10SwiftGodot8ViewportC25anisotropicFilteringLevelAC011AnisotropicE0Ovp","role":"symbol","roleHeading":"Instance Property"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"final","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"anisotropicFilteringLevel","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Viewport","preciseIdentifier":"s:10SwiftGodot8ViewportC","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Viewport"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"AnisotropicFiltering","preciseIdentifier":"s:10SwiftGodot8ViewportC20AnisotropicFilteringO","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/Viewport\/AnisotropicFiltering"},{"text":" { ","kind":"text"},{"text":"get","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"set","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" }","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The anisotropic filtering level also affects decals and light projectors if they are configured to use anisotropic filtering. See "},{"code":"ProjectSettings\/rendering\/textures\/decals\/filter","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"ProjectSettings\/rendering\/textures\/lightProjectors\/filter","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In 3D, for this setting to have an effect, set "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/BaseMaterial3D\/textureFilter-swift.property"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/BaseMaterial3D\/TextureFilter-swift.enum\/linearWithMipmapsAnisotropic"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/BaseMaterial3D\/TextureFilter-swift.enum\/nearestWithMipmapsAnisotropic"},{"type":"text","text":" on materials."}]}]},{"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In 2D, for this setting to have an effect, set "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasItem\/textureFilter-swift.property","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasItem\/TextureFilter-swift.enum\/linearWithMipmapsAnisotropic","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasItem\/TextureFilter-swift.enum\/nearestWithMipmapsAnisotropic","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" on the "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasItem","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" node displaying the texture (or in "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasTexture","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"). However, anisotropic filtering is rarely useful in 2D, so only enable it for textures in 2D if it makes a meaningful visual difference."}]}]}]}],"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"Sets the maximum number of samples to take when using anisotropic filtering on textures (as a power of two). A higher sample count will result in sharper textures at oblique angles, but is more expensive to compute. A value of ","type":"text"},{"code":"0","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" forcibly disables anisotropic filtering, even on materials where it is enabled.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/viewport\/anisotropicfilteringlevel"]}],"references":{"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/BaseMaterial3D/TextureFilter-swift.enum/nearestWithMipmapsAnisotropic":{"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/basematerial3d\/texturefilter-swift.enum\/nearestwithmipmapsanisotropic","type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"nearestWithMipmapsAnisotropic"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The texture filter reads from the nearest pixel and blends between 2 mipmaps (or uses the nearest mipmap if "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ProjectSettings\/rendering\/textures\/defaultFilters\/useNearestMipmapFilter"},{"type":"text","text":" is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"true"},{"type":"text","text":") based on the angle between the surface and the camera view. This makes the texture look pixelated from up close, and smooth from a distance. Anisotropic filtering improves texture quality on surfaces that are almost in line with the camera, but is slightly slower. The anisotropic filtering level can be changed by adjusting "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ProjectSettings\/rendering\/textures\/defaultFilters\/anisotropicFilteringLevel"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/BaseMaterial3D\/TextureFilter-swift.enum\/nearestWithMipmapsAnisotropic","title":"BaseMaterial3D.TextureFilter.nearestWithMipmapsAnisotropic","role":"symbol"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/CanvasItem/TextureFilter-swift.enum/linearWithMipmapsAnisotropic":{"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/canvasitem\/texturefilter-swift.enum\/linearwithmipmapsanisotropic","type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"linearWithMipmapsAnisotropic"}],"abstract":[{"text":"The texture filter blends between the nearest 4 pixels and blends between 2 mipmaps (or uses the nearest mipmap if ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ProjectSettings\/rendering\/textures\/defaultFilters\/useNearestMipmapFilter"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"true"},{"text":") based on the angle between the surface and the camera view. This makes the texture look smooth from up close, and smooth from a distance. Anisotropic filtering improves texture quality on surfaces that are almost in line with the camera, but is slightly slower. The anisotropic filtering level can be changed by adjusting ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ProjectSettings\/rendering\/textures\/defaultFilters\/anisotropicFilteringLevel"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasItem\/TextureFilter-swift.enum\/linearWithMipmapsAnisotropic","title":"CanvasItem.TextureFilter.linearWithMipmapsAnisotropic","role":"symbol"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/CanvasItem":{"kind":"symbol","title":"CanvasItem","abstract":[{"text":"Abstract base class for everything in 2D space.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/CanvasItem","fragments":[{"text":"class